Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 18:13:17 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 12/12] buffer pools: the pool buffer is still the pool's while
 it is destroyed

clReleaseContext aborted on macOS (reference_tracked_object.h:137,
"current < 0") as soon as the context had a small-buffers pool: the
context destroys its pools, a pool destroys its buffer (mainStorage, a
unique_ptr), and MemObj::~MemObj asks the context whether the buffer is
a pool buffer (Context::isPoolBuffer compares mainStorage.get()) to
know whether it holds a context reference to drop. libstdc++ deletes
the object and then clears the pointer; libc++ clears the pointer
first, so with libc++ the answer was "no", the buffer dropped a
reference it never took and the count went below zero.
AbstractBuffersPool now deletes its buffer before it clears the
pointer, which gives the same answer with either library.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 shared/source/utilities/buffer_pool_allocator.h | 10 +++++++++-
 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/shared/source/utilities/buffer_pool_allocator.h b/shared/source/utilities/buffer_pool_allocator.h
index a76aab8ccb..5dac59a3ac 100644
--- a/shared/source/utilities/buffer_pool_allocator.h
+++ b/shared/source/utilities/buffer_pool_allocator.h
@@ -46,7 +46,15 @@ struct AbstractBuffersPool : public NonCopyableClass {
     AbstractBuffersPool(MemoryManager *memoryManager, OnChunkFreeCallback onChunkFreeCallback, const SmallBuffersParams &params);
     AbstractBuffersPool(AbstractBuffersPool &&bufferPool) noexcept = default;
     AbstractBuffersPool &operator=(AbstractBuffersPool &&other) noexcept = delete;
-    virtual ~AbstractBuffersPool() = default;
+    virtual ~AbstractBuffersPool() {
+        // The buffer must still be the pool's while it is destroyed: MemObj::~MemObj asks the context whether it is a
+        // pool buffer (Context::isPoolBuffer: mainStorage.get() == buffer) to know whether it holds a context
+        // reference. libc++'s unique_ptr clears its pointer before it deletes the object (libstdc++'s after), so the
+        // answer was "no" with libc++ (macOS), the context's internal reference count went below zero and
+        // clReleaseContext aborted. Delete first, clear afterwards, with either library.
+        delete mainStorage.get();
+        static_cast<void>(mainStorage.release());
+    }
 
     void tryFreeFromPoolBuffer(BufferParentType *possiblePoolBuffer, size_t offset, size_t size);
     bool isPoolBuffer(const BufferParentType *buffer) const;
